The period from 1458 to 1538 covers the eighty years between the first Zong sighting of the Hachina coast and the conclusion of the [[Treaty of Anping]]. It is the period in which Hachinawa was transformed by contact, and it ended with the state militarily dominant over every neighbour, diplomatically recognised by a foreign empire, and administered more closely than at any time in its history.

No Zong colony existed on Hachina soil in this period and no Zong force campaigned against the state. The change was commercial: gold left Hachinawa and weapons, horses, pigs, paper and luxuries entered it, in a trade conducted without the authorisation of the Zong colonial government and concealed by traders who recorded the goods as lost or destroyed.

== First contact ==

Zong explorers reached the Hachina coast in 1458, coasting north from [[Anping]] to survey the shoreline, and the encounter is recorded from both sides.

The Hachina came out to the ships, were amazed by them, and gave offerings. Communication was minimal. What is known of their reasoning comes from their own account, and it is unusually clear.

The strangers were not taken for gods. The Hachina had observed that the Zong ships carried captured people of the region, whose speech resembled their own, and concluded from this that the strangers were people who had conquered people of their kind and had come from the sea. They classified them accordingly — as equals, but of the sea, against the Hachina's own strength, which was of the land.

The naming followed the same logic. The Zong were Welanawa, the western realm; the Hachina were Hachinawa, the middle realm. A state that classifies a foreign arrival as a peer empire on first sight has a conception of itself as a polity and a diplomatic vocabulary already in place, and the naming is the clearest single indication that Hachinawa was a state in the full sense when contact came.

== Unrecognised alliance ==

For some forty years after contact, the two powers understood their relationship differently, and neither was deceiving the other.

The Hachina believed themselves to be in an alliance — a distant understanding with a great power against mutual enemies. The Zong, whose interest in the coast was an anchorage and a source of provisions on a long voyage, paid the relationship little mind, did not regard Hachinawa as a threat, and did not consider conquest of it worth the distance.

The misunderstanding did not become a dispute, because it did not become anything: the alliance amounted to nothing material until the sixteenth century. The Zong coasted the shoreline and called at Hachina safely, the Hachina did not attack them, and the relationship remained one of convenience on one side and of significance on the other.

== Gold and arms ==

The trade that transformed Hachinawa began in the later fifteenth century and was, from the Zong side, illegal.

Hachinawa's gold came from its tributaries, principally [[Uwisa]] and [[Yelani]], whose highlands hold the highest ground in Sinchew. The gold was collected as tribute and accumulated at the court, where before contact it had little to be spent on that would alter the balance of power.

Contact gave it a market. Individual Zong explorers and traders sold firearms and cannon to the Hachina without the authorisation of the colonial government, at exorbitant prices, and concealed the transactions by reporting the weapons as lost or destroyed. The proceeds returned to the Zong colony of [[Louyang Colony|Louyang]], where they funded a growing arms manufacturing industry.

The effect on Hachinawa was immediate and structural:

* Gold bought weapons.
* Weapons permitted the conquest and enforcement of the upland tributaries, and the prosecution of the long war with the [[Kawesa]] over access to the gold-bearing hills.
* The submission of the tributaries produced more gold, which bought more weapons.

The cycle gave Hachinawa means of coercion that its grain surplus had not. Firearms permitted the taking of fortified upland positions, and horses permitted the movement of force into country that infantry could not cross quickly enough to be effective. Neither was available to its neighbours.

=== Terms of trade ===

The prices at which the trade was conducted bore no relation to what the goods cost to produce.

Iron, pigs and paper were ordinary commodities in Zong, produced in quantity and cheaply. Gold and silver were not produced in Zong at all, and reached the western coast only as cargo. The Hachina, having no iron-working of their own and no paper, had no means of judging what the goods should cost, and the traders who set the prices had every reason not to tell them.

The result was an exchange in which small quantities of ordinary goods were paid for with large quantities of precious metal. The traders who conducted it accumulated gold and silver in a short time on a scale that the ordinary commerce of the coast could not produce, and it was this rather than the sale of weapons that made the fortunes of the period. The firearms were the most consequential export; they were not the most profitable.

The trade is recorded in the Zong papers chiefly through the audits that eventually caught it, and the quantities of precious metal reported as having entered the colony in the 1490s and 1500s exceed what the colony's own commerce could account for.

The Hachina court reached the same conclusion. By the early sixteenth century the quantity of gold leaving the country in exchange for goods of ordinary manufacture had become a matter of record at court, and the comparison between what was paid and what was received was one the scribes could make from the tribute rolls and the trade accounts by the 1520s. It became one of the standing grievances of the reign, argued among the great houses of the nawe, that the state was paying the price of its own ascendancy to foreigners who were selling it common goods.

=== Horses ===

Horses reached Hachinawa by the same route as the firearms, carried to the [[Sinchew|western coast]] on Zong vessels. They were not bred in the region, and their supply depended on the trade as completely as the supply of powder did.

Their effect was greatest on the state's relations with its upland tributaries. A horse can carry a rider and his weapons faster than men can walk, and can cover the distance that separates the plain from the Uwisa country in a fraction of the time an infantry force requires. The state acquired, for the first time, a means of reaching a tributary that had ceased to pay and of doing so before news of its approach preceded it.

Horses are poorly suited to forest and to steep ground, and their military value in [[Sinchew]] was therefore confined to the plain, to the roads, and to the lower slopes of the uplands. What they gave Hachinawa was not the ability to occupy the mountains but the ability to reach the valleys and the passes at their feet at a speed the peoples of those places could not match.

=== Pressure on Uwisa ===

The principal development of the later part of the period was the increase in the demands made of the Uwisa.

Gold was the object. The tributary gold of [[Uwisa]] and [[Yelani]] was the resource on which the whole arrangement rested: it paid for the firearms and the horses, and it was collected as tribute rather than produced by the state. As the court's requirements grew, so did the assessment, and the later records of the period show the gold demanded of the Uwisa rising while the quotas set for the other tributaries changed little.

That the increase could be enforced at all followed from the weapons and the horses. Before them, a tributary that refused to pay could be punished by a campaign that took months to organise and longer to carry out, and which the tributary saw coming. With them, the state could move on a district within days of a default, and the terms it could impose were correspondingly harder.

The tribute registers of the sixteenth century record the assessment of the Uwisa house by house. Whether the population of the Uwisa country declined over the same period, and whether the higher assessments were met or evaded, the surviving records do not establish.

== Livestock ==

The only domesticated animal in Hachinawa before contact was the turkey, which was kept for meat and, in the uplands, for feathers. The plain had no herd animal and no beast of burden: cultivation was done by hand, and goods moved by boat and by porter.

Pigs came with the trade, and became the most popular of the imports. They suited the country in ways the turkey did not. A pig will live on the waste of a grain economy — the broken grain, the chaff, the refuse of the household and the market — and so turns the by-product of the harvest into meat without competing with people for the grain itself. In a plain that grew cereals, the pig was the animal that cost least to keep.

They spread quickly through the countryside and the towns, and the later records of the period list them as a household animal rather than a rarity. By the sixteenth century pork was a commodity of the state's own commerce, and it is the currency in which Hachinawa later paid for the service of its allies — the terms of the [[Second Mahik War]] included the supply of 25,000 warriors in exchange for pork and iron.

Horses and pigs between them changed the state's relationship to animals: the one gave it speed it had not had, the other a source of meat that required no new land.

== Paper ==

The most consequential import of the period was not a weapon.

Paper reached Hachinawa through the same trade, and it changed the state's administration. The Hachina had possessed a logographic script for some seven centuries, together with a solar calendar and a system of numerals, and had kept records — but the medium had been the monument and the inscribed stone, which suits the proclamation and the dedication rather than the ledger.

Paper allowed the record to be voluminous, portable and disposable. What had been carved in public was now written in quantities: tribute rolls naming every tributary house and the gold assessed on it, grain accounts by district and year, registers of land and of obligation, and correspondence with client rulers that could be kept and consulted rather than remembered.

The earliest Hachina paper records date from the late fifteenth century, and the tribute registers of the sixteenth are the fullest administrative documents to survive from pre-colonial Sinchew.

== Society and culture ==

The eighty years of contact were a period of rapid change in which the forms of the older state persisted while everything they contained was altered.

At court, the influx of luxuries transformed display. Zong ceramics, silks and metalwork entered the gift economy of the great houses, and the objects became markers of standing in the competition among the landholding lineages from which the nawe was drawn. Consumption became a form of political statement.

In the army, firearms displaced the weapons of the older order. The chronicles of the period record the first cannon in the reign of Tsulahik's predecessors and the general adoption of the firearm in the field within two generations. The change was not merely tactical: a king whose power rested on gunpowder depended less on the levies of the great houses and more on the treasury that bought the powder, which strengthened the crown against the lineages that elected it.

In religion and ceremony, the calendar and the agricultural cycle continued to govern the year, and the king continued to open and close it. No cult was displaced by contact. What changed was the scale of the state's ceremonial capacity, funded by tribute and gold.

Among the tributaries, the terms of submission hardened. Where the relationship had once fluctuated — a strong king collected regularly, a weak one in name — the firearm made enforcement cheap, and the gold and labour demanded of Uwisa and Yelani increased over the period. Whether [[Nahola]] was also tributary, and how the three ranked against one another, is not settled by the surviving records.

In the countryside, the plain itself changed least. The districts continued to be taxed in grain by officials appointed from the capital, the canals continued to be maintained, and the villages continued to plant by the calendar. The transformation of Hachinawa in this period was a transformation of its court, its army and its administration, not of its agriculture.

=== Literacy and the schools ===

Hachinawa maintained institutions for the training of the officials who administered the grain and the calendar, and these existed before contact.

They were attached to the temple complexes and to the royal household, and their function was practical rather than scholarly. A student learned the script, the numerals, the calendar and the arithmetic required to assess a harvest, keep a granary account and record an obligation. The men they produced were the district officials who measured the grain and kept the tribute rolls, and the priests who fixed the dates of sowing, irrigation and harvest by the calendar.

Establishments of this kind had existed since the script itself, though they became larger and more numerous as the state's administration grew. They are the institutions that the Zong would later have recognised as schools, and the reason the court was able to absorb new learning quickly: there were places in which a new subject could be taught.

Literacy was concentrated at the top of the society, among the royal household, the priestly lineages, the great landholding houses and the officials in their service. It was not a general accomplishment. But the class that possessed it was large, and it grew over the period: the state's officials, priests, scribes and the households of the great lineages together constituted a numerous elite by the time the Zong arrived.

That elite is what the court drew upon in the sixteenth century. The men who learned Zong were drawn from the schools and the great houses; the men who compiled the trade accounts from the 1520s were trained in establishments older than the trade itself. The administrative system that made the period's records possible was not created by contact — it was there to be used.

=== Population ===

Estimates for the population of Hachinawa before contact are constructed from the area of the plain and from the density of comparable agrarian states. The Hachina territory comprises about 328,196 km², of which some seventy-nine per cent is uniform arable ground in a single elevation band, and the population supported by that agriculture is estimated at about 1.2 million in the middle of the fifteenth century.

The figure is higher than that of any other people of Sinchew by an order of magnitude, and the difference is structural rather than accidental: the other families occupy rainforest, upland or coastal terrain with no comparable cereal base. It is also the reason the Hachina could field 45,000 men in the [[First Mahik War]] without emptying the country, and the reason the Zong estimated the Hachina as a power rather than a people.

The returns of the period are not administrative censuses of the kind the Zong colony kept from 1540, but tribute and grain rolls, from which totals can be inferred rather than read. No figure is given in any surviving document.

== Learning Zong ==

The court's answer to the terms of trade, and to its dependence on the traders who set them, was to learn the language of the people it was dealing with.

The king and the court engaged merchants out of the Zong colonies to teach ''Zong'' to Hachina students, and paid them for it. The arrangement was not a school: teachers were hired individually by the crown and by the great houses, and instructed the sons of the court and of the principal lineages, together with scribes and officials who were to be employed in the trade.

The programme had two objects. The first was commercial — a Hachina who could read a Zong bill of lading and conduct a negotiation in the traders' own language was not dependent on the traders' account of what a cargo was worth. The second was diplomatic — a court that could correspond directly with the colonial administration at [[Anping]], without intermediaries whose interests were their own, could deal with the Zong as a state rather than through the merchants who came to its coast.

By the 1530s the court had a body of men competent in written and spoken Zong. It was this corps, rather than the traders, that conducted the diplomacy of the treaty period: the embassy that [[Nachiha]] led to [[Anping]] in 1538 was drawn from it, and the negotiation of the [[Treaty of Anping]] was carried on in Zong by Hachina officials who understood what the documents in front of them said.

The programme is also the reason the Hachina record of the period is as full as it is. The accounts of the trade, of the tribute and of the negotiations of the 1530s were written, in part, by men trained for the purpose, and the paper they wrote on came from the same trade they were describing.

== Approach to the treaty ==

By the 1530s the relationship had altered on both sides.

The Zong colonial administration, having failed for decades to find a route north, concluded that a harbour on the northwestern coast was necessary and selected the site at Tamsui. Hachinawa was the power that could prevent or permit it.

For Hachinawa, the calculation was different. The state had spent eighty years becoming the strongest power in the region, and the Zong were the source of the means by which it had done so. An agreement with them formalised what the trade had already made true.

The [[Treaty of Anping]] was concluded in 1538. Hachinawa recognised the Zong as holding a fixed coastal presence; the Zong recognised the Hachina king as a sovereign and renounced claims to the interior. The treaty also provided for the joint campaign against the Kawesa that became the [[First Mahik War]].

